cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  reliablock: redundancy arithmetic that is explicit about the assumption doing
  the work
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-10-25
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  Takes a declared reliability block diagram -- series, parallel, k-of-n,
  standby with a declared switching reliability, shared load, common cause as a
  declared beta factor, and a general two-terminal network -- with per-block
  failure distributions, and computes system reliability over a mission profile
  in closed form, minimal cut sets, and Birnbaum, criticality and
  Fussell-Vesely importance measures. The headline output is the reliability
  computed with and without the declared common-cause beta, side by side,
  because independence is the assumption that fails in flight hardware and the
  one most often left implicit. On a thirteen-block small-satellite bus over
  26298 hours the declared beta factors of 0.12, 0.08 and 0.05 move the system
  unreliability from 3.049573463e-02 to 3.771048369e-02, a ratio of 1.236582,
  and raise the number of minimal cut sets of order one from 3 to 6 -- the
  second half being the part that is not a percentage, because a group whose
  members share a cause is not redundant against that cause however many
  members it has. There is no learned component, deliberately: for a declared
  diagram with declared distributions the reliability has a correct answer, so
  a fitted model could only approximate the arithmetic. Every structure is
  checked against an independent reference -- the binomial survival function
  from scipy.stats for k-of-n, a convolution integral from scipy.integrate.quad
  for standby, a four-state Markov ODE solved with DOP853 at rtol 1e-12 for
  shared load, exhaustive state enumeration for the Boolean structures -- with
  a worst relative difference of 4.038264e-14 across 108 shared-load cases and
  4.281257e-16 across 300 k-of-n cases. Minimal cut sets agree with exhaustive
  enumeration on all 13 diagrams measured, over 147712 states. Birnbaum
  importance agrees with a central finite difference to 3.490329e-09 over 135
  comparisons spanning five decades of step size. The five-component bridge
  network's closed form R = 2p^5 - 5p^4 + 2p^3 + 2p^2 is derived in full in
  reliablock.bridge by pivotal decomposition and verified five independent
  ways; no page number or published figure is quoted for it, because this build
  had no access to the volumes cited for the method. Monte Carlo budgets are
  sized from a declared target interval half-width of 1 percent of the system
  unreliability rather than chosen as round numbers, and all 8 runs lie within
  1.9129 standard errors of their closed form. Numerically, every small failure
  probability is carried in unreliability space: the complementary route
  1 - prod(1 - q_i) returns exactly zero for 10 of 18 measured parallel cases
  where the answer is positive, and the naive series expression falls outside
  an independently derived Bonferroni bracket in 13 of 20 cases, which a
  property test sharing the expression could not detect. Hypothesis found three
  library defects the hand-written suite did not, all pinned as named
  regression tests. 454 tests pass; 134 validation checks pass and 1 fails, the
  failure being a published measurement that the cumulative-exposure ageing
  model diverges from calendar-time hazard integration by up to 1852.73 percent
  for a non-exponential block, which is a modelling choice and not a defect.
  The reliability package (0.9.0) is the better tool for distribution fitting,
  accelerated life testing and repairable-system analysis and the README says
  so; it is not installed in the environment these numbers were measured in and
  no number here came from it. Validation is level 2. Research grade. Not
  flight-qualified, not certified, not approved for operational aerospace use.
  A reliability number is not a safety case.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - reliability-block-diagram
  - redundancy
  - common-cause-failure
  - beta-factor
  - minimal-cut-sets
  - importance-measures
  - birnbaum
  - fussell-vesely
  - aerospace-assurance
references:
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Barlow
        given-names: "R. E."
      - family-names: Proschan
        given-names: "F."
    title: "Statistical Theory of Reliability and Life Testing"
    publisher:
      name: "Holt, Rinehart and Winston"
    year: 1975
    notes: >-
      Chapter 1, the coherent structure function and its pivotal decomposition,
      which is the method reliablock.quantify uses for a general two-terminal
      network and the method the bridge closed form is derived by in
      reliablock.bridge. Cited for the METHOD only. No page number and no
      numerical value in this repository is taken from it; the bridge
      polynomial is derived in full in reliablock.bridge's module docstring and
      verified five independent ways in validation/validate_bridge.py.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Billinton
        given-names: "R."
      - family-names: Allan
        given-names: "R. N."
    title: "Reliability Evaluation of Engineering Systems"
    edition: "2nd"
    publisher:
      name: "Plenum Press"
    year: 1992
    notes: >-
      The bridge network and the conditional-probability method applied to it.
      Cited for the method only; this build had no access to the volume and no
      page number or published figure is quoted from it.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Esary
        given-names: "J. D."
      - family-names: Proschan
        given-names: "F."
    title: "Coherent structures of non-identical components"
    journal: "Technometrics"
    volume: 5
    issue: 2
    year: 1963
    notes: >-
      The min-cut lower and min-path upper bounds on the reliability of a
      coherent system, implemented as reliablock.bounds.esary_proschan_bounds
      and used as an independently derived check that shares no expression with
      the quantifier. The bounds are exact for a series-parallel structure and
      strict for the bridge, where the measured bracket at p = 0.9 is
      [9.781407801000e-01, 9.973487799000e-01], of width 1.920800e-02, around
      an exact value of 0.97848.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Bonferroni
        given-names: "C. E."
    title: >-
      Teoria statistica delle classi e calcolo delle probabilita
    journal: >-
      Pubblicazioni del R. Istituto Superiore di Scienze Economiche e
      Commerciali di Firenze
    volume: 8
    year: 1936
    notes: >-
      The first- and second-order inequalities on the probability of a union,
      implemented as reliablock.bounds.bonferroni_series_bounds. Both sides are
      sums of non-negative terms, so neither inherits the cancellation in
      1 - prod(1 - q_i), which is why this is the check that detects it: the
      naive expression falls outside the bracket in 13 of 20 measured cases.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Birnbaum
        given-names: "Z. W."
    title: >-
      On the importance of different components in a multicomponent system
    year: 1969
    notes: >-
      In Multivariate Analysis II, Academic Press, pages 581 to 592. The
      Birnbaum importance measure dQ_sys/dq_i, implemented in
      reliablock.importance and verified against a central finite difference of
      the system unreliability to 3.490329e-09 over 135 comparisons.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Fussell
        given-names: "J. B."
    title: >-
      How to hand-calculate system reliability and safety characteristics
    journal: "IEEE Transactions on Reliability"
    volume: "R-24"
    issue: 3
    year: 1975
    notes: >-
      The Fussell-Vesely importance measure. Computed exactly here, by
      enumerating the states of the events appearing in the relevant cut sets,
      rather than by the rare-event approximation, which was measured to
      overestimate by up to 1.268384e-01 on the diagrams in
      validation/validate_importance_output.txt and is the usual source of a
      published Fussell-Vesely figure above 1.
  - type: report
    authors:
      - family-names: Fleming
        given-names: "K. N."
    title: >-
      A reliability model for common mode failures in redundant safety systems
    institution:
      name: "General Atomic"
    number: "GA-A13284"
    year: 1975
    notes: >-
      The origin of the beta factor. Cited for the model's structure only. No
      numerical beta in this repository comes from any source: every one is a
      declared input chosen to span a range, and the README says so.
  - type: report
    authors:
      - family-names: Mosleh
        given-names: "A."
      - family-names: Fleming
        given-names: "K. N."
      - family-names: Parry
        given-names: "G. W."
      - family-names: Paula
        given-names: "H. M."
      - family-names: Worledge
        given-names: "D. H."
      - family-names: Rasmuson
        given-names: "D. M."
    title: >-
      Procedures for Treating Common Cause Failures in Safety and Reliability
      Studies
    number: "NUREG/CR-4780, EPRI NP-5613"
    year: 1988
    notes: >-
      The standard treatment of parametric common-cause models, which sets the
      beta factor in the context of the multiple-Greek-letter and alpha-factor
      models that carry a separate parameter for each size of partial
      common-cause failure. Those are NOT implemented here, and
      validation/validate_commoncause_output.txt measures where their absence
      matters: for a k-of-n group with k >= 2 the beta factor stops being
      conservative above a member cumulative hazard located by bisection, at
      0.302944 for 2-of-3 and 0.042978 for 5-of-6 at beta = 0.1. Structure
      only; no numerical value in this repository is taken from it.
  - type: standard
    title: >-
      IEC 61508-6:2010, Functional safety of electrical, electronic and
      programmable electronic safety-related systems, Part 6
    year: 2010
    notes: >-
      Annex D, the beta-factor quantification used in functional-safety
      practice. Structure only; no numerical value in this repository is taken
      from it, and nothing in this repository is a functional-safety argument.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Weibull
        given-names: "W."
    title: "A Statistical Distribution Function of Wide Applicability"
    journal: "Journal of Applied Mechanics"
    volume: 18
    issue: 3
    year: 1951
    notes: >-
      The Weibull life model, implemented as reliablock.distributions.Weibull
      and cross-checked against scipy.stats.weibull_min to 1e-12 relative on
      the survival function.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Nelson
        given-names: "W."
    title: >-
      Accelerated Testing: Statistical Models, Test Plans, and Data Analyses
    publisher:
      name: "Wiley"
    year: 1990
    notes: >-
      Chapter 10, the cumulative-exposure model, which is the ageing model
      reliablock.mission implements for a stress factor. It is exact for an
      exponential block and an approximation otherwise, and
      validation/validate_mission.py declares and FAILS a check that it agrees
      with calendar-time integration of a scaled hazard rate, with a measured
      worst divergence of 1852.73 percent. Structure only; no numerical value
      in this repository is taken from it.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Wilson
        given-names: "E. B."
    title: >-
      Probable Inference, the Law of Succession, and Statistical Inference
    journal: "Journal of the American Statistical Association"
    volume: 22
    year: 1927
    notes: >-
      The score interval for a binomial proportion, used for every Monte Carlo
      interval in this repository because it stays inside [0, 1] and does not
      collapse to zero width when no failure is observed -- both of which
      matter for a redundant diagram whose unreliability is small by
      construction.
  - type: software
    title: "reliability"
    version: "0.9.0"
    notes: >-
      The mature alternative, and the better choice for distribution fitting,
      accelerated life testing and repairable-system analysis. Named and
      recommended for those in this package's README. NOT installed in the
      environment these numbers were measured in, and no number in this
      repository came from it or was cross-checked against it; that is recorded
      as a deviation in validation/VALIDATION.md.
  - type: software
    title: "lifelines"
    notes: >-
      Survival analysis and regression on lifetime data with censoring, which
      is the right tool when the input is field or test data rather than a
      declared diagram. Named in this package's README. NOT installed in the
      environment these numbers were measured in, and no number in this
      repository came from it.
